[{:db/id "q-79282/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" :answer/id #uuid "fcfdbc49-db48-5f8b-b297-bec0d4d3ee2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" :answer/id #uuid "54d1d4a0-f8f6-5672-abf9-0f1ace6e2ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" :answer/id #uuid "3cce299e-c723-54e3-be6a-ed178d0c440f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282/selection/42113895f4a3b82bea25716b9e3a345a8aece1d1f1e571b15045782992436230" :answer/id #uuid "8a8e774d-d440-5960-8e0c-244b3853f579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e" :answer/id #uuid "dff2e200-f496-56a8-8fb7-69d45115db29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1969e976-d8a6-5726-a203-06c93d7f2686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79282/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" :answer-field/choices ["q-79282/selection/254f7dc7c93945f5d800c08002268b921573aada24b982a69940059f541d28cc" "q-79282/selection/4205b99be199dc7950356b855ff4a5477c48c73878a7b026d25c035dbf46acb5" "q-79282/selection/5bda35dc7fabc5f84da2118d36bbe7c90786c71810c1d882ae9f6c6935b490ae" "q-79282/selection/42113895f4a3b82bea25716b9e3a345a8aece1d1f1e571b15045782992436230" "q-79282/selection/83d70e0048515696e5c8563bea8d4b2c3cdf15ab2aa67edacd2c421ea082130e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79282" :question/id #uuid "b63b1e1e-81bd-51c3-84ae-72e1137b3714" :question/math-academy-id "q-79282" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/1617a8efd9110fa435dc396d90450c7fe17d18105e2358ab5610135e32a385ad.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,-1),$ $a=4$ and $b=2.$  Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/4659fda20ed9a2b5e5c10d60f504e4db378efb0bfe9ae037f4998511e75300cb.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79282/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79282"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-79285"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/5fb18775e625816f119cf95c09051eba41fced187f4e4d1e7964921f4b877288.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106704"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/5bd3bf7cecf806ab0fc89db59afc5d385064080f47076d13d2ef25650ce39a23.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-327809/field-1/a9f1cfdfdf58b277c54e080bc1ce6e173896b8f18cfa005ec45645b28344367c" :answer/id #uuid "6965f472-60e1-58d9-b012-8d39b8d0be10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{81}+\\frac{y^2}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327809/field-1/57126c0cffd277349efc4f0d7b7419e98df0ccd17bb42aacfcabf8dd5e88b20d" :answer/id #uuid "b8d59c10-32e0-5912-b9cf-4e4eaea6e549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{48}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327809/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "00e0e8ab-f5df-517b-b20c-b0d83df16c90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327809/field-1/a9f1cfdfdf58b277c54e080bc1ce6e173896b8f18cfa005ec45645b28344367c" :answer-field/choices ["q-327809/field-1/a9f1cfdfdf58b277c54e080bc1ce6e173896b8f18cfa005ec45645b28344367c" "q-327809/field-1/57126c0cffd277349efc4f0d7b7419e98df0ccd17bb42aacfcabf8dd5e88b20d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327809" :question/id #uuid "0c2b92e3-65f3-50ac-b60f-da322c49e8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-327809" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-327809/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327809"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-327835"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/6f983fda4328227558e46aaaec454db2ccc0611331495c923ad1d90c7cb3df8a.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065811/assets/101d9b26027bc5667009b0c7af69fa190c987c362122402fce62daec5833092f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
